# .dockerignore (repository root) — REPO-BASELINE.md §1, §9:
# "Write the .dockerignore as EXCLUSION-based for a monorepo: the backend image build
#  must not ship web/, tests/ or the infra tree as build context."
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHICH BUILD THIS FILE GOVERNS
#
# Docker reads .dockerignore from the CONTEXT ROOT. Exactly one image in this repository
# has the repository root as its context:
#
#     src/AbOvo.Api/Dockerfile      context = repo root   (flyio/api.fly.toml: context = "..")
#
# The web image does NOT read this file. Its context is the pnpm workspace at web/ (see
# flyio/web.fly.toml and the header of web/app/Dockerfile), so its rules live under web/.
# Excluding `web/` below therefore costs the frontend build nothing — it is not reading
# this file, and it would not be shipping this tree either way.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THE API BUILD NEEDS, AND WHICH MUST THEREFORE SURVIVE EVERY RULE BELOW
#
#     global.json                                  the SDK pin
#     Directory.Build.props                        TFM, warnings-as-errors, NuGetAudit
#     Directory.Packages.props                     central package management
#     src/AbOvo.Api/                               the service
#     src/AbOvo.ServiceDefaults/                   referenced project
#     src/AbOvo.Contracts/                         referenced project
#
# Central package management is why the two props files are not optional: the .csproj
# files carry no Version attribute, so a restore that cannot see Directory.Packages.props
# fails on every package rather than on one. Check any new rule against this list before
# committing it — the failure mode is a COPY that cannot find a path, and the message
# names the path without saying that an ignore rule is why it is absent.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE LOAD-BEARING TWO
#
# `**/bin/` and `**/obj/` are not tidiness. Without them the source COPY overwrites the
# container's restore output with the HOST's obj/project.assets.json, whose packageFolders
# point at a path that does not exist in the image — and `dotnet publish --no-restore` then
# fails on packages it was just told were restored. flyio/README.md records the same thing.

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 1. Build output — the load-bearing exclusions
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/.vs/
**/TestResults/
**/artifacts/
**/*.binlog
**/*.user
**/*.suo

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2. Whole trees the backend image has no use for
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# This is the §1 rule itself. Every one of these would otherwise be uploaded to the
# builder on every build, invalidate the cache on every unrelated edit, and — for the
# frontend tree — dwarf the thing being built.

# The frontend. Its own image is built from web/ as context; this build never reads it.
web/

# Tests. Nothing in the runtime image runs them, and the API Dockerfile never COPYs them.
tests/

# Documentation, ADRs, diagrams, papers.
docs/

# The book's own content — the product's source material, served at runtime from the
# database, never baked into the API image.
content/

# The infrastructure tree. fly.toml files describe how the built image is deployed; they
# are read by flyctl, not by the build.
flyio/

# Repository automation and agent definitions.
.github/
.claude/

# Onboarding and operational scripts. The image runs `dotnet AbOvo.Api.dll` and nothing else.
scripts/

# The development-only composition root (P1). The API Dockerfile restores the PROJECT and
# not the solution precisely so the Aspire SDK never enters this build; keeping AppHost out
# of the context makes that structural rather than incidental.
src/AbOvo.AppHost/

# The solution file, for the same reason: `dotnet restore src/AbOvo.Api/AbOvo.Api.csproj`
# walks the ProjectReference graph itself and never wants a solution that includes AppHost.
AbOvo.sln
AbOvo.slnx

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3. Node and frontend artefacts, wherever they are
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# web/ is already excluded wholesale. These are the belt to that braces: a stray
# node_modules anywhere under src/ (a tool, an experiment) must not become a layer.
**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/out/
**/.turbo/
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.pnpm-store/
**/coverage/
**/playwright-report/
**/test-results/

# The documentation tooling at the repository root — markdownlint, the link checker, the
# Mermaid renderer. It is a Node package that happens to live beside a .NET solution, it
# ships in no image, and nothing in src/ references it. Listing it costs a line and keeps
# the rule above honest: `docs/` being excluded does not by itself exclude the tools that
# lint it.
/package.json
/package-lock.json
/.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4. Secrets — REPO-BASELINE.md §2, P5
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# A secret reaches a container through the platform, never through a build context. A
# build context becomes an image layer, and an image layer is readable by anyone who can
# pull the image. A .env that was never meant to leave a laptop must not become one.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12
**/secrets.env
secrets.env.example
**/.fly/

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5. Version control, editors, OS noise
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# .git is excluded deliberately and safely: nothing in this build reads git metadata.
# There is no SourceLink package in Directory.Packages.props, and no build step derives a
# version from a commit. If one is ever added, this line is what will have to change —
# and the symptom will be a version stamped `0.0.0` rather than a failure.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.editorconfig
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/.idea/
**/.vscode/
**/*.swp
**/*~

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6. Documentation at the root
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
*.md
LICENSE
.gitleaks.toml

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# NOT excluded, on purpose: Dockerfiles.
#
# The obvious rule here is `**/Dockerfile`, and it is left out. The Dockerfile is named
# on the command line with -f, and whether a given builder reads it from the filesystem or
# from the uploaded context is a property of the builder, not of this repository — flyctl,
# BuildKit and the classic builder have not always agreed. The upside of excluding it is
# four kilobytes; the downside is a deploy that fails with "failed to read dockerfile" on
# one builder and works on another. Not worth it.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
